---
import ToolLayout from '@/layouts/ToolLayout.astro'
import VideoToMp3Batch from '@/components/tools/media/VideoToMp3Batch.tsx'
---

<ToolLayout slug="mkv-to-mp3">
  <VideoToMp3Batch
    client:load
    from="mkv"
    hint="Multi-gigabyte Matroska files are fine. Only the audio is read from them"
  />
  <Fragment slot="explainer">
    <h2>Why MKV audio extraction is worth doing locally</h2>
    <p>
      MKV files are large: concert recordings, conference talks, archived broadcasts, Blu-ray rips.
      Upload converters often refuse them. A free tier with a 500 MB cap cannot take a 6 GB Matroska
      file, and an upload of six gigabytes to extract 150 MB of audio wastes your connection.
    </p>
    <p>
      To convert MKV to MP3 here, the tool never decodes the video. FFmpeg reads the audio stream out
      of the container, re-encodes it with LAME and writes an MP3. The video track (usually more than
      95% of the file) is discarded without being decoded, so extraction is much faster than a
      conversion.
    </p>

    <h2>What FFmpeg finds inside a typical MKV</h2>
    <div class="overflow-x-auto">
      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th>Audio codec in the MKV</th>
            <th>Where it comes from</th>
            <th>Converts to MP3?</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr>
            <td>AAC</td>
            <td>Most re-encodes and web sources</td>
            <td>Yes</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>AC-3 / E-AC-3</td>
            <td>Broadcast and DVD sources</td>
            <td>Yes, downmixed to stereo</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>DTS</td>
            <td>Blu-ray sources</td>
            <td>Yes, downmixed to stereo</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>FLAC</td>
            <td>Lossless music rips</td>
            <td>Yes. Use 320 kbps, because the source is lossless</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>Opus / Vorbis</td>
            <td>WebM-derived and open-source encodes</td>
            <td>Yes</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>PCM</td>
            <td>Capture cards and recorders</td>
            <td>Yes</td>
          </tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>

    <h2>Multiple audio tracks</h2>
    <p>
      People choose Matroska because it can hold six audio tracks and a commentary. This page takes the
      <strong>first</strong> stream, which is the default track in almost all files: the original
      language in a film, the room mic in a recording. To choose track four needs stream mapping, a
      complex command-line operation, and this page does not offer it. For that job, use FFmpeg on a
      desktop.
    </p>

    <h2>Surround sound becomes stereo</h2>
    <p>
      In practice MP3 is a stereo format, so a 5.1 AC-3 or DTS track is downmixed to two channels.
      Dialogue from the centre channel goes into both sides, which suits headphones and phone speakers.
      If you need to keep the discrete channels, MP3 is the wrong format.
    </p>

    <h2>How much smaller is the result?</h2>
    <p>
      A two-hour 4 GB film with a 192 kbps MP3 soundtrack gives about <strong>165 MB</strong>, about
      one twenty-fifth of the original. The arithmetic is bitrate × duration: 192 kbps × 7,200 seconds
      ÷ 8 bits ÷ 1,024 ÷ 1,024. The page shows the before and after size of each file it converts.
    </p>

    <h2>Doing it offline</h2>
    <p>
      After the first download from this site, the FFmpeg engine stays cached. Put the browser in
      offline mode, or disconnect, and all conversions on this page still work. That shows your file
      stays on your device.
    </p>
  </Fragment>
</ToolLayout>
